hello hello congratulations yet again  thank you  all right so let's talk about your  history with pixar a little bit can you  name something about your approach to  scoring a pixar movie that has stayed  consistent from film to film but then  what's something about lightyear that  posed a unique one-of-a-kind challenge  for you  well i mean the the  the process is always the same whether  it's live action or animation and it's  about understanding the characters and  understanding the story and  understanding what these people are  going through  um it's it's it's you know  a lot of people think oh it's animated  so then you must do a particular thing  because it's animated and the truth is i  don't do anything different on a pixar  movie than i would do on a uh you know  mission impossible movie or a star trek  movie or a or a thing it's it's all  about  you know uh embracing these characters  understanding who they are  and why they do what they do and what  are the what are the trials and  tribulations and what are the struggles  and  the the sadness that they're going  through at the time it's like really  embracing that because i think that's  the only way to understand them as  humans the only way to connect to  somebody is to understand them and and  put yourself in their in their world in  their place so so that's always the  first thing um  and then the unique challenge on this  particular one which was different than  any of the other pixars i had to do was  the way we recorded it you know it was  done during the pandemic we weren't  allowed to be together  everything had to be done over zoom  i was recording musicians in a in a  studio with the musicians couldn't even  be together normally all the players  would be together close to each other so  the way we had to do this was separate  all the sections so you would have the  brass section who again normally would  be next to each other spread out six to  ten feet apart plastic sheets between  them they couldn't hear each other  properly  we had to find new ways to to mic the  room to get the sound we wanted because  it was for for years you've only done it  one way you know and that is to have  everyone together have all the things  happening at once and then suddenly we  were faced with this process of wait you  cannot do that you have to do it  differently how are you going to do it  and still get the same result and that  was a massive challenge but uh  i have a great team of people and warren  brown who was the engineer on this he he  killed it and we spent a lot of time in  the mix  really tweaking and figuring out how do  we get this to sound like it the way we  used you know uh so that was an enormous  challenge i think for everybody but uh  but we you know we got through it and  and and it worked out we learned a lot  in the process too so well that was  going to be my next question because as  someone who's always desperately  reaching for silver linings and  challenging situations is there anything  about kind of turning the process on its  head in that respect that you know  made you find like a corner of this  process or or a certain technique that  you never would have realized was usable  before that now you could take on to  future films even without copic  restrictions yeah i think so i think  there was a lot in terms of  understanding just technically space and  sound and how it projects and how it  works and how you can then do that but  more importantly i think it was one of  those things that in the beginning  you're like oh how is this ever going to  work it's not going to work  the real lesson is it can work  you just have to  make it work you know and and and when  an obstacle is in front of you you just  have to uh embrace the obstacle and make  it put a part of your team  and and make it work and i think that  you know in terms of what you can learn  from what we've all been through in the  last couple years is how did we adapt  you know and did you willingly adapt or  did you begrudgingly adapt and if you  willingly adapt and you ran forward  straight into it i felt like  you learned something about yourself and  i think i think that was something for  all of us that we you know it was uh and  we probably won't have the full  understanding of it for years to come  but uh you know yeah it was an  interesting way to go about things i  think about that quite often all right  so one thing about this movie that i'm  totally obsessed with is the idea of it  being a movie within a movie because you  know essentially what lightyear is to  andy that's what star wars and the mcu  movies are to us so considering you've  actually scored so many of those movies  is there anything from those scores that  you found influencing what you were  doing on light year so it had the same  effect on andy oh yeah it was a complete  love letter to all the movies i grew up  with watching absolutely so so the the  the music you hear in light years  is all taken from the years of  everything that i've listened to and  watched over the over the many years of  of you know consuming this sort of  entertainment which i love so much  uh it's absolutely all related to that  uh  but tied up in a nice new bow to put out  into the world so uh yeah there's a  there's a laundry list of movies i could  go through that that that are apart that  all at the heart and soul of light year  you know and i think you know and  everyone on the team felt the same way  about diving into it that way and it  certainly started with angus who is also  a massive fan of all of this stuff and  and being such friends with angus over  the years i i met him uh before the  incredibles actually worked on one man  band together  and uh and since then we've had so many  geeky conversations about every movie  you can imagine and this was just a  giant extension of that so  i'll wrap up with this one it's a it's a  bit of a broader question but obviously  if you you've worked on some of the  biggest film franchises out there you've  won an oscar you want an emmy and then  some but do you have a score that you're  just especially proud of that was maybe  for a movie that flew kind of under the  radar that you wish more people out  there were aware of  you know in that there's a couple of  them uh and it's it's not just the the  music that i'm proud of it's the movie  itself the movies you know like i always  feel like john carter got a bad rap i  feel like speed racer got a bad rap and  those two those are  those are two movies i absolutely love  and i feel like you know and i know that  they have their fan base they have their  ardent supporters which i'm very very  happy about uh but sometimes you know  you  you it's not you don't work any less on  a movie and work any less hard on a  movie uh everything you do is really  hard and you're always trying to make  the best thing you can uh so when it  doesn't quite hit the mark the way  you're hoping or when it's not received  the way you are you're always thinking  about you know god so many people worked  so hard on this thing and  and uh but the silver lining is all  those people that are that that are out  there in the world and through twitter  and the internet now you you know them  you know that it was embraced in a  certain way  uh and over time things get looked at  differently so  um those are probably two that come to  my head i think it doesn't really fall  into that category but inside out isn't  just my favorite pixar score it's one of  my favorite scores of all time oh thank  you that's quite that one i still  listened to it over and over thank you  so much i loved working on that film  pete doctor's the best  seriously one of the best of the best  this one is excellent as well huge  congratulations on this and everything  you've accomplished hope we get to talk  again soon thank you so much
 

